.TH ULIB80 1 "November 2025" "um80 0.3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ulib80 \- Microsoft LIB-80 compatible library manager for 8080/Z80
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ulib80
.B \-c
.I library.lib
.I file.rel
[\fIfile.rel\fR]...
.br
.B ulib80
.B \-l
.I library.lib
.br
.B ulib80
.B \-p
.I library.lib
.br
.B ulib80
.B \-x
.I library.lib
[\fImodule\fR]...
.br
.B ulib80
.B \-a
.I library.lib
.I file.rel
[\fIfile.rel\fR]...
.br
.B ulib80
.B \-d
.I library.lib
.I module
[\fImodule\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ulib80
is a Microsoft LIB-80 compatible library manager.
It creates and maintains libraries of relocatable object modules (.REL files).
The
.BR ul80 (1)
linker uses these libraries.
.PP
A library collects commonly used routines in one file.
When ul80 links a program, ul80 includes in the output only the modules
that satisfy undefined references.
This keeps executables small.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v\fR, \fB\-\-version
Display the version information and exit.
.PP
Specify exactly one of these operations:
.TP
.B \-c
Create a new library from the specified .REL files.
If the library file exists, ulib80 overwrites the library file.
.TP
.B \-l
List the modules in the library.
Show the module names and the sizes.
.TP
.B \-p
Print the public symbols that each module of the library defines.
.TP
.B \-x
Extract modules from the library.
If you give no module name, ulib80 extracts all modules.
ulib80 writes each extracted module to the file \fImodule\fR.rel.
.TP
.B \-a
Add .REL files to an existing library.
The library must already exist.
.TP
.B \-d
Delete the named modules from the library.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I library.lib
The library file to operate on.
By convention, library files have the .lib extension.
.TP
.I file.rel
Relocatable object files (.REL files) to add to the library.
.TP
.I module
Module name, without the .rel extension, for extraction or deletion.
.SH LIBRARY FORMAT
The ULIB format of ulib80 has these parts:
.IP \(bu 2
Magic header "ULIB"
.IP \(bu 2
Version number
.IP \(bu 2
Module directory with names, offsets, and public symbol lists
.IP \(bu 2
Concatenated module data
.PP
The ULIB format is specific to ulib80.
The ULIB format differs from the original Microsoft LIB-80 format, but the
ULIB format has the same purpose.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a library:
.B ulib80 \-c mylib.lib io.rel math.rel string.rel
.TP
List library contents:
.B ulib80 \-l mylib.lib
.TP
Show public symbols:
.B ulib80 \-p mylib.lib
.TP
Extract all modules:
.B ulib80 \-x mylib.lib
.TP
Extract specific module:
.B ulib80 \-x mylib.lib math
.TP
Add a module:
.B ulib80 \-a mylib.lib newmod.rel
.TP
Delete a module:
.B ulib80 \-d mylib.lib oldmod
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The operation succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
The operation failed because of errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I library.lib
The library archive file.
.TP
.I module.rel
The relocatable object file.
.SH TYPICAL WORKFLOW
.RS
.nf
# Assemble source files
um80 \-o io.rel io.mac
um80 \-o math.rel math.mac
um80 \-o string.rel string.mac

# Create library
ulib80 \-c stdlib.lib io.rel math.rel string.rel

# Link with library
ul80 \-o program.com main.rel stdlib.lib
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR um80 (1),
.BR ul80 (1),
.BR ucref80 (1),
.BR ud80 (1)
.PP
The original Microsoft LIB-80 manual is in the retro_docs archive,
https://github.com/avwohl/retro_docs/blob/main/um80_and_friends/cref_lib.pdf
.SH NOTES
.IP \(bu 2
ulib80 derives each module name from the name of the .REL file, without the
extension.
ulib80 converts the module name to uppercase.
.IP \(bu 2
A library cannot contain duplicate module names.
.IP \(bu 2
When the linker searches a library, the linker includes only the modules that
define symbols that other modules reference.
.SH AUTHOR
um80 project contributors.
